WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.949 --> 00:00:02.949
Film Courage: How do you write suspense?

00:00:02.949 --> 00:00:06.880
Van Ditthavong, Filmmaker/Photographer: Again
painfully.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:13.690
I think it’s setting up that world.

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:18.470
For me I love setting up that world maybe
because I was a photographer and love and

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:28.960
I love where I can describe the room, describe
the sound and then I know for me terror isn’t

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:30.000
in your face.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.060
It’s like if things are quiet and then all
the sudden you start to hear my voice and

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:43.250
then suddenly you hear a cup crash in the
other room and we both turn, that’s scary

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:47.080
to me versus music.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:57.440
I try to…I thinking pacing is a great part
of it and I know to have the audience want

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:05.530
something that they don’t want to see but
are curious about so you try to lead them

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:12.780
on as long as possible before you frustrate
the audience by doing it.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:14.960
I’m still working on it.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:20.149
Hopefully the next couple ones will be more
suspenseful but it’s a work in progress

00:01:20.149 --> 00:01:23.530
I guess in terms of getting the right “Ahh!”

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:30.340
Or like on the edge of the seat feeling so
still working on that craft.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:34.289
Film Courage: What did ALL ROADS TO PEARLA
teach you about pacing?

00:01:34.289 --> 00:01:36.329
Van: It’s everything.

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:46.249
It’s rhythm, it’s how you get into a story,
it’s how you leave, you can be in what part

00:01:46.249 --> 00:01:53.420
too long, one scene too long, the dialogue
can go on a little too much, so it’s like

00:01:53.420 --> 00:02:02.249
being a DJ, it’s like you have to know when
to start the crowd, when to get them going,

00:02:02.249 --> 00:02:09.849
when to settle them down, when to exit and
that comes through I guess experience and

00:02:09.849 --> 00:02:16.400
trying to which thankfully I was able to make
this one and learn from issues I might have

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:22.500
faced and wasn’t so successful on and try
to get better at that but pacing is absolutely

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.220
everything.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:35.530
The editing is everything and it’s too that
right, it’s like rhythms again, it’s like

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:36.530
music.

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:38.950
I see that in composition too.

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:42.790
Rhythms is super important.

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:45.450
Film Courage: How do you know when to leave
a scene?

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:50.030
Van: Again, I think that’s subjective in
taste.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:57.930
So you build your taste of things that you
like so I would take it like if I was watching

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:01.220
this film how would I feel?

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:08.879
If it’s too long it’s really being an
honest assessment of yourself and having fresh

00:03:08.879 --> 00:03:14.790
eyes on it and helps other people who understand
what you’re trying to do and take those

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:16.689
criticism because man this is we don’t need
this.

00:03:16.689 --> 00:03:17.279
Questions For The Viewers: When you think
of pacing, what movie do you think of first?

